n doing  that type of testing in this  neighborhood because the area was once  home to at least 14 chemical plants that  release lead waste our soil sample came  back at over 11  hundred over 1,100 yeah which is close  to three times the limit for play spaces  for kids recent town hall meetings have  been packed with parents of small  children who are concerned about the  various ways lead can spread reporter  Wendy Ruderman worked on the  Philadelphia Inquirer Daily News  investigation she says unregulated  construction sites may be contributing  to the problem we found lots of sites  with just giant heaping piles of dirt  left uncovered blowing in the wind even  if people have low levels of lead dr.  Thomas Farley is Philadelphia's health  commissioner the most important thing is  to be wetting the material so the dust  isn't generated when they demolish the  houses have the contractors been doing  that we know that the contractors are  not following those recommendations now  or those requirements now after the  interview a spokeswoman for the city  said Parks and Rec facilities near a  construction site will now be hosed down  several times a week signs will be  posted at all Parks and Rec facility  sites reminding kids to wash their hands  and contractors have been sent letters  reminding them of the city's air  management quality laws but dr. Farley  says lead levels and children outside of  the river wards area are even higher we  think that the primary source of  exposure for lead in children today is  the interior paint nationwide the CDC  reports that lead paint may account for  up to 70% of the elevated lead levels  there may very well be some contribution  from soil but that doesn't appear to be  the major source but any source says  father of three bill pace is worthy of  the city's attention if you really want  to eradicate lead poisoning in the kids  in the city you have to take away a  hundred percent not seventy and to  ignore the 30% I think is a huge mistake  no one's parents replace the topsoil and  now mop twice a day to avoid further  exposure Nolan's blood level is now at a  four and she's taking iron supplements  her parents say she is doing better but  they're still worried bottom line there  are unsafe levels of soil in many urban  neighborhoods across the country doctors  say knowing about the  and testing your children early are the  best ways to prevent lead poisoning  Thanks a lot of good information here  Thank You jurika

